1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,889
Velocity, plus variety and veracity, make it a genuine big data problem.

2
00:00:07,467 --> 00:00:16,570
Virtual reality is a computer generated three dimensional world that replaces what you see and hear, so you feel present inside it.

3
00:00:16,570 --> 00:00:17,521
It is a loop.

4
00:00:17,521 --> 00:00:27,560
Sensors track your head and hands; the engine draws two images, one per eye, for depth; the headset displays them; you move again.

5
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:33,860
The loop must close in under about twenty milliseconds, ninety times a second, or the user feels sick.

6
00:00:35,067 --> 00:00:37,204
Place the technologies on a line.

7
00:00:37,204 --> 00:00:38,832
The plain real world.

8
00:00:38,832 --> 00:00:44,509
Augmented reality adds computer objects to your real view, like Pokémon Go.

9
00:00:44,509 --> 00:00:48,415
Mixed reality anchors virtual objects to real ones.

10
00:00:48,415 --> 00:00:52,464
Virtual reality replaces the world altogether.

11
00:00:52,464 --> 00:01:01,136
VR itself is non immersive on an ordinary screen, semi immersive on a big projection, or fully immersive in a tracked headset.

12
00:01:02,367 --> 00:01:14,152
Applications: virtual labs, surgical training and therapy, walking through a building before it exists, flight simulators where crashing is free, and heritage tours.

13
00:01:14,152 --> 00:01:20,895
The drawbacks: expensive hardware, motion sickness, eye strain, and content that is costly to make.

14
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:23,986
Why VR needs so much bandwidth.

15
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:33,299
Two eyes, two thousand one hundred and sixty squared pixels each, twenty four bits per pixel: two point two four times ten to the eight bits per frame.

16
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:36,872
Ninety frames a second: about twenty gigabits per second.

17
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:43,018
No home network carries that, so the picture is drawn inside the headset or heavily compressed.

18
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:54,578
e-commerce is buying and selling, and moving the money, over electronic networks.

19
00:01:54,578 --> 00:01:57,404
Its models are named by who sits at each end.

20
00:01:57,404 --> 00:02:01,375
Business to business: a wholesaler supplying shops.

21
00:02:01,375 --> 00:02:05,281
Business to consumer: an online shop like Daraz.

22
00:02:05,281 --> 00:02:09,956
Consumer to consumer: selling your old bicycle on Hamrobazar.

23
00:02:09,956 --> 00:02:15,307
Consumer to business: a freelancer selling photos to a company.

24
00:02:15,307 --> 00:02:23,041
In Nepal the money moves through wallets and QR payments, under the Electronic Transactions Act of twenty sixty three.

25
00:02:24,267 --> 00:02:36,808
e-medicine, or telemedicine, delivers health care at a distance: video consultation, X rays and ECGs sent to a specialist, wearable monitors, electronic records.

26
00:02:36,808 --> 00:02:45,844
For Nepal the argument is geography: a Kathmandu specialist can advise a health post in Humla in minutes instead of a two day journey.

27
00:02:45,844 --> 00:02:54,718
Its limits: no physical examination, it needs bandwidth and power, and patient privacy and responsibility are hard questions.

28
00:02:55,934 --> 00:03:02,537
e-governance is government using ICT to serve citizens, businesses and its own offices.

29
00:03:02,537 --> 00:03:14,607
Four types: government to citizen, like the Nagarik App; government to business, like online VAT filing; government to government, like a shared national ID database; and government to employee.

30
00:03:14,607 --> 00:03:21,448
It brings transparency and fewer queues, but faces the digital divide and cyber security risks.

31
00:03:26,234 --> 00:03:31,353
Mobile computing lets the user, the device and the connection all move.

32
00:03:31,353 --> 00:03:36,105
It needs mobile hardware, mobile software and wireless communication.

33
00:03:36,105 --> 00:03:50,182
Each network generation was ten to a hundred times faster than the last: analogue voice in the eighties, SMS on 2G, mobile internet on 3G, video on 4G, and about ten gigabits per second on 5G.

34
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:53,160
How much faster is 5G?

35
00:03:53,367 --> 00:03:56,636
First convert to bits: twenty eight thousand megabits.

36
00:03:56,867 --> 00:04:01,129
On 4G: fourteen hundred seconds, twenty three minutes twenty.

37
00:04:01,334 --> 00:04:05,977
On 5G: one hundred and forty seconds, ten times faster.

38
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:13,886
For live uses like remote surgery, the drop in delay from fifty to five milliseconds matters even more.

39
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:27,711
The internet of things, a phrase Kevin Ashton coined in nineteen ninety nine, connects physical objects fitted with sensors and software so they exchange data and can be controlled remotely.

40
00:04:27,711 --> 00:04:29,378
It has four layers.

41
00:04:29,378 --> 00:04:31,683
Perception: the sensors.

42
00:04:31,683 --> 00:04:34,430
Network: the gateway and the link.

43
00:04:34,430 --> 00:04:37,282
Processing: the cloud that decides.

44
00:04:37,282 --> 00:04:40,251
Application: the app the farmer sees.

45
00:04:40,251 --> 00:04:46,707
Its weakest point is security: cheap devices shipped with default passwords have been hijacked into botnets.

46
00:04:47,934 --> 00:04:50,874
Why IoT needs IPv6.

47
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:55,225
IPv4 gives about four point three billion addresses.

48
00:04:55,434 --> 00:04:59,396
IPv6 gives three point four times ten to the thirty eight.

49
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:04,784
IoT devices alone would need four point seven times all of IPv4.

50
00:05:04,784 --> 00:05:08,684
With IPv6, the shortage simply disappears.

51
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:12,671
Pause and think.

52
00:05:19,967 --> 00:05:25,518
Software as a service: finished software in a browser, and you manage only your data.

53
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:27,935
One more.

54
00:05:35,567 --> 00:05:36,819
Virtualisation.

55
00:05:36,819 --> 00:05:41,268
It is the technology under cloud computing, not a V of big data.

56
00:05:43,467 --> 00:05:44,588
Quick recap.

57
00:05:44,588 --> 00:05:49,627
AI and its nested layers, and robots that sense, decide and act.

58
00:05:49,627 --> 00:05:52,114
Cloud service and deployment models.

59
00:05:52,114 --> 00:05:54,185
The five Vs of big data.

60
00:05:54,185 --> 00:05:56,698
Virtual and augmented reality.

61
00:05:56,698 --> 00:05:59,627
The e-commerce and e-governance models.

62
00:05:59,627 --> 00:06:01,982
And the four layers of the internet of things.

63
00:06:06,167 --> 00:06:09,228
And that completes Class 12 Computer Science.

64
00:06:09,228 --> 00:06:12,262
Well done for making it through all seven units.

65
00:06:12,262 --> 00:06:13,452
See you in the next subject.

